{
  "dom_id": "step-q275328",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $1$ and $2$ with probabilities $0.3$ and $0.7,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=5,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=8\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$",
  "worked_solution": "We are asked to compute the expected value of $X,$ given conditional expectations of $X$ based on the value of another random variable $Y.$ This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=5$\n\n- $Y=2$ with probability $0.7,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=8$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.3+8\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =1.5+5.6 \\\\  & =7.1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
